Treat Android as Linux in platform detection.

Node on Android reports process.platform === 'android'. VS Code only ever
compares against 'linux', so on device isLinux, isWindows and isMacintosh are all
false at once and every Linux-shaped decision falls through to an untested
default. Nothing errors; the behaviour is just quietly wrong.

The one that is easiest to observe: terminal profiles are stored under
`terminal.integrated.profiles.linux`, and the workbench picks that suffix from
the platform the remote reports. With none matching, the whole profile block is
skipped and terminals fall back to $SHELL with no arguments -- measured on an API
36 emulator, where an explicit --init-file placed in the profile args never
reached the spawned shell.

isAndroidPlatform is exported alongside it because collapsing the two is wrong in
exactly one place: which target platform the extension marketplace is asked for.
Patch 0009 uses it there; everything else wants isLinux. It is deliberately not
called isAndroid -- that name is already taken further down the same file by a
user-agent test asking whether the browser showing the workbench is Android,
which on a phone is also true and is a different question.

This is the first of the Android adaptations to move from a regex against
minified output to a real source patch. `git apply` fails loudly when the context
shifts, which is the point: the regexes it replaces print SKIP and exit 0.

diff --git a/src/vs/base/common/platform.ts b/src/vs/base/common/platform.ts
index d2072de..c211d65 100644
--- a/src/vs/base/common/platform.ts
+++ b/src/vs/base/common/platform.ts
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@ let _isWindows = false;
 let _isMacintosh = false;
 let _isLinux = false;
 let _isLinuxSnap = false;
+let _isAndroidPlatform = false;
 let _isNative = false;
 let _isWeb = false;
 let _isElectron = false;
@@ -74,7 +75,15 @@ declare const navigator: INavigator;
 if (typeof nodeProcess === 'object') {
 	_isWindows = (nodeProcess.platform === 'win32');
 	_isMacintosh = (nodeProcess.platform === 'darwin');
-	_isLinux = (nodeProcess.platform === 'linux');
+	// Android reports its platform as 'android', not 'linux'. This single line
+	// decides isLinux, Platform, OS and getTargetPlatform for the whole product,
+	// and everything downstream that asks "is this a Linux-shaped system" -- path
+	// separators, shell selection, the platform-suffixed settings keys -- is
+	// answered from here. Bionic is close enough to Linux for all of them.
+	_isLinux = (nodeProcess.platform === 'linux' || nodeProcess.platform === 'android');
+	// Kept apart from isLinux because one decision does have to tell the two
+	// apart: which target platform the extension marketplace is asked for.
+	_isAndroidPlatform = (nodeProcess.platform === 'android');
 	_isLinuxSnap = _isLinux && !!nodeProcess.env['SNAP'] && !!nodeProcess.env['SNAP_REVISION'];
 	_isElectron = isElectronProcess;
 	_isCI = !!nodeProcess.env['CI'] || !!nodeProcess.env['BUILD_ARTIFACTSTAGINGDIRECTORY'] || !!nodeProcess.env['GITHUB_WORKSPACE'];
@@ -143,6 +152,11 @@ export const isWindows = _isWindows;
 export const isMacintosh = _isMacintosh;
 export const isLinux = _isLinux;
 export const isLinuxSnap = _isLinuxSnap;
+// The OS this process runs on. Not to be merged with the isAndroid further
+// down, which asks whether the *browser* showing the workbench is Android --
+// on a phone both are true, but they answer different questions and only
+// this one may decide anything about the server.
+export const isAndroidPlatform = _isAndroidPlatform;
 export const isNative = _isNative;
 export const isElectron = _isElectron;
 export const isWeb = _isWeb;
